 succeeded in 0ms:
     1	# Moving-Throat PDE — Stage 122: Concrete Mouth-Source Branch and Compensation Test
     2	
     3	## Goal
     4	
     5	Compute the normalized mouth-coupling ratio
     6	\[
     7	\mathfrak g=\frac{g_q\sqrt{K_s}}{g_s\sqrt{K_q}}
     8	\]
     9	on the first explicit local mouth-source branch, and test whether that branch already lands on the canonical compensation family.
    10	
    11	## 1. Natural equal-normalized local mouth source
    12	
    13	Take one local mouth source profile that couples to the shell and the mixed channel with equal strength **after normalization by their quadratic channel weights**:
    14	\[
    15	\boxed{
    16	g_s=g_m\sqrt{K_s},
    17	\qquad
    18	g_q=g_m\sqrt{K_q}.
    19	}
    20	\]
    21	This is the simplest concrete local branch with **no channel favoritism** at the mouth.
    22	
    23	Then
    24	\[
    25	\boxed{
    26	\mathfrak g_{\rm nat}=1.
    27	}
    28	\]
    29	
    30	This should be read as a concrete branch ansatz, not as a theorem of the full PDE: it is the cleanest “same mouth source, same normalized loading” core closure.
    31	
    32	## 2. Exact compensation family
    33	
    34	Stage 221 already showed that the compensated canonical outgoing branch requires
    35	\[
    36	1+\mathfrak r^2=4(\mathfrak g-\mathfrak r)^2,
    37	\]
    38	equivalently
    39	\[
    40	\boxed{
    41	\mathfrak g_{\rm comp}^{\pm}(\mathfrak r)
    42	=
    43	\mathfrak r\pm\frac12\sqrt{1+\mathfrak r^2}.
    44	}
    45	\]
    46	
    47	Inserting the Stage 223 geometric value
    48	\[
    49	\mathfrak r=\mathfrak r_{F1}=\frac{\sqrt{4107-100\pi^2}}{10\pi}
    50	\]
    51	gives the two exact compensated mouth-coupling values
    52	\[
    53	\boxed{
    54	\mathfrak g_{\pm}^{F1}
    55	=
    56	\frac{2\sqrt{4107-100\pi^2}\pm 37\sqrt3}{20\pi}.
    57	}
    58	\]
    59	
    60	Numerically,
    61	\[
    62	\boxed{
    63	\mathfrak g_-^{F1}\approx 0.758035078944663,
    64	\qquad
    65	\mathfrak g_+^{F1}\approx 2.79795199200529.
    66	}
    67	\]
    68	
    69	## 3. Test of the natural branch
    70	
    71	The natural equal-normalized mouth branch gives
    72	\[
    73	\mathfrak g_{\rm nat}=1.
    74	\]
    75	That is **not** exactly on the compensation family at \(\mathfrak r=\mathfrak r_{F1}\), since
    76	\[
    77	1\neq \mathfrak g_-^{F1},
    78	\qquad
    79	1\neq \mathfrak g_+^{F1}.
    80	\]
    81	
    82	Equivalently, the exact compensation defect evaluated on the natural branch is
    83	\[
    84	\mathcal C_{\rm nat}
    85	:=
    86	1+\mathfrak r_{F1}^2-4(1-\mathfrak r_{F1})^2
    87	=
    88	\frac{-12321+80\pi\sqrt{4107-100\pi^2}}{100\pi^2}
    89	\approx 1.74016524722739,
    90	\]
    91	so the natural branch misses the compensated surface.
    92	
    93	## 4. How far away is the nearest compensated branch?
    94	
    95	The **near** compensated branch is the minus branch, since
    96	\[
    97	|\mathfrak g_{\rm nat}-\mathfrak g_-^{F1}|
    98	<
    99	|\mathfrak g_{\rm nat}-\mathfrak g_+^{F1}|.
   100	\]
   101	Numerically,
   102	\[
   103	\boxed{
   104	\Delta \mathfrak g_- = 1-\mathfrak g_-^{F1}\approx 0.241964921055337,
   105	}
   106	\]
   107	while
   108	\[
   109	\boxed{
   110	\Delta \mathfrak g_+ = \mathfrak g_+^{F1}-1\approx 1.79795199200529.
   111	}
   112	\]
   113	
   114	So the natural equal-normalized mouth source is **not** automatically compensated, but it is much closer to the lower compensated branch than to the upper one.
   115	
   116	## 5. Exact traction renormalization factors
   117	
   118	Since Stage 221 gave
   119	\[
   120	\mathfrak g \propto \frac{1}{\mathcal T_m},
   121	\]
   122	the traction amplitude required to move from the natural branch \((\mathfrak g=1)\) to the compensated branches is
   123	\[
   124	\boxed{
   125	\frac{\mathcal T_m^{(\pm)}}{\mathcal T_m^{\rm nat}}
   126	=
   127	\frac{1}{\mathfrak g_\pm^{F1}}.
   128	}
   129	\]
   130	
   131	Numerically,
   132	\[
   133	\boxed{
   134	\frac{\mathcal T_m^{(-)}}{\mathcal T_m^{\rm nat}}
   135	\approx 1.31920016339112,
   136	\qquad
   137	\frac{\mathcal T_m^{(+)}}{\mathcal T_m^{\rm nat}}
   138	\approx 0.357404273860789.
   139	}
   140	\]
   141	
   142	So the nearest compensated branch is reached by a **31.9% traction enhancement** relative to the simplest equal-normalized mouth-source branch.
   143	
   144	## Result
   145	
   146	The first truly concrete mouth-core test is now complete:
   147	
   148	- the actual throat geometry fixes
   149	  \[
   150	  \mathfrak r_{F1}\approx 1.778;
   151	  \]
   152	- the simplest equal-normalized local mouth source gives
   153	  \[
   154	  \mathfrak g_{\rm nat}=1;
   155	  \]
   156	- this does **not** lie exactly on the compensation surface;
   157	- but the nearest compensated branch is only a modest traction renormalization away.
   158	
   159	So the surviving ambiguity is no longer broad outlet algebra. It is whether the real moving-throat mouth source is exactly equal-normalized, or instead slightly biased toward the lower compensated branch.

